   "directness": "Direct semiconductor wafer-start bottleneck",
   "scopeNote": "<strong>Process boundary:</strong> This node covers the crystal-growth stage in which electronic-grade silicon is melted, seeded and pulled into a monocrystalline ingot. The analysis includes feedstock quality, quartz crucibles, puller and hot-zone control, crystal yield, defect management and customer qualification. Subsequent slicing, lapping, etching and polishing are treated in the next wafer-finishing stage.",
   "basics": [
    [
     "What it is",
     "The Czochralski process melts electronic-grade silicon in a high-purity crucible, introduces a precisely oriented seed crystal and slowly pulls a cylindrical monocrystalline ingot from the melt. Temperature gradients, pulling speed, rotation, dopant concentration and hot-zone stability must all remain tightly controlled. Small deviations can create defects that reduce downstream wafer yield or prevent customer qualification."
    ],
    [
     "Why Artificial Intelligence matters",
     "Artificial Intelligence (AI) accelerators, advanced processors and memory systems all begin with qualified semiconductor wafers. Crystal quality at the ingot stage influences wafer flatness, defect density, epitaxial performance, lithography yield and ultimately device reliability. The node is therefore a direct enabling input to advanced semiconductor manufacturing, even though it remains upstream from chip fabrication itself."
    ],
    [
     "Supply-chain role",
     "This stage converts highly purified silicon feedstock into a controlled single-crystal ingot suitable for wafer manufacture. It sits after electronic-grade polysilicon preparation and before slicing, lapping, etching, polishing and final wafer qualification. Its economics are shaped by process yield, customer qualification, diameter capability and utilisation rather than by raw-material price alone."
    ],
    [
     "Dominant geography",
     "Japan, Taiwan, Germany, South Korea, the United States and China are central to semiconductor-wafer manufacturing and related crystal-growth capability. The industry remains concentrated among technically established suppliers because customer qualification is lengthy and failure costs are high. Geographic diversification is strategically important, but announced capacity has limited value until it demonstrates stable yield and customer acceptance."
    ]
   ],
